# Plain Text as a Publishing Format

I like content that remains useful even when the software around it disappears.

That is one of the strongest arguments for keeping writing in plain text.

A text file can be opened almost anywhere. It can be searched with ordinary tools, stored in Git, reviewed as a diff, copied between systems, and understood without launching a particular application.

## Separate Content From Presentation

When content is stored separately from its presentation, the same source can be rendered in different ways.

A short article might become an HTML page today and part of an archive tomorrow. The underlying text does not need to change just because the presentation does.

This separation also makes redesigns easier. Changing a stylesheet is much simpler than rewriting years of content to fit a new page structure.

## Human-Readable Source

I think source files should be pleasant to read directly.

A lightweight markup language works well because formatting remains visible without overwhelming the writing itself.

For example, a heading still looks like a heading. A link is still recognizable as a link. The document does not become a wall of tags or configuration.

That makes the source useful beyond the build tool that processes it.

## Version Control Becomes More Useful

Plain text works especially well with Git.

Changes are easy to inspect. A rewritten sentence appears as a rewritten sentence rather than a change buried inside serialized application data.

For a personal site, that means the history of the repository can also become the history of the writing.

## Durable by Default

Publishing systems come and go.

Text files are unlikely to become difficult to open.

That durability is one reason I prefer generating conventional web output rather than requiring a runtime application to serve every page.

The same principle applies to the final site, which I discuss in [Building Smaller Websites](::building-smaller-websites).